"Data from: Predicting and improving complex beer flavor through machine learning | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/23624915)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://zenodo.org/records/23624915\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 321\nL0: Published October 24, 2026 | Version v4\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Data paper】 Open\nL3: \nL4: # Data from: Predicting and improving complex beer flavor through machine learning\nL5: ### Authors/Creators\nL6: \nL7:   * 【1†Schreurs, Michiel (Researcher)】\nL8:   * 【2†Piampongsant, Supinya (Researcher)】\nL9:   * 【3†Roncoroni, Miguel (Researcher)^{1}】\nL10:   * 【4†Cool, Lloyd (Researcher)^{1}】\nL11:   * 【5†Malaver, Beatriz Herrera (Researcher)^{1}】\nL12:   * 【6†Vanderaa, Christophe (Researcher)^{2}】\nL13:   * 【7†Thesseling, Florian (Researcher)^{1}】\nL14:   * 【8†Kreft, Lukasz (Researcher)^{3}】\nL15:   * 【9†Botzki, Alexander (Work package leader)^{3}】\nL16:   * 【10†Malcorps, Philippe (Researcher)^{4}】\nL17:   * et al. 【11†Show all 13 authors】\nL18: Authors/Creators\nL19: \nL20: [Input: Search by name or affiliation…]\nL21: \nL22: 13 / 13\nL23:   * 【1†Schreurs, Michiel (Researcher)】\nL24:   * 【2†Piampongsant, Supinya (Researcher)】\nL25:   * 【3†Roncoroni, Miguel (Researcher)^{1}】\nL26:   * 【4†Cool, Lloyd (Researcher)^{1}】\nL27:   * 【5†Malaver, Beatriz Herrera (Researcher)^{1}】\nL28:   * 【6†Vanderaa, Christophe (Researcher)^{2}】\nL29:   * 【7†Thesseling, Florian (Researcher)^{1}】\nL30:   * 【8†Kreft, Lukasz (Researcher)^{3}】\nL31:   * 【9†Botzki, Alexander (Work package leader)^{3}】\nL32:   * 【10†Malcorps, Philippe (Researcher)^{4}】\nL33:   * 【12†Daenen, Luk (Researcher)^{4}】\nL34:   * 【13†Wenseleers, Tom (Work package leader)^{2}】\nL35:   * 【14†Verstrepen, Kevin J. (Project leader)^{1}】\nL36:   * 1. VIB-KU Leuven Center for Microbiology\nL37:   * 2. KU Leuven\nL38:   * 3. VIB Bioinformatics Core\nL39:   * 4. Anheuser-Busch InBev (Belgium)\nL40: ## Description\nL41: \nL42: This repository contains all the supplemental files and scripts for the Nature Communications paper 'Predicting and Improving Complex Beer Flavor through Machine Learning'.\nL43: Init_modeling.py\nL44: \nL45: Helper script that contains some functions for model performance evaluation and storage.\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: Jupyter Notebook for training machine learning models.ipynb\nL50: Jupyter notebook containing the code to train the 10 different machine learning models of the research article, including hyperparameter optimization and model performance evaluation.\nL51: \nL52: The 10 models included are:\nL53: \nL54:   * Linear regression\nL55:   * Lasso regression\nL56:   * Partial least squares regression\nL57:   * Random Forest regression\nL58:   * Extra-trees regression\nL59:   * Adaboosted regression\nL60:   * Gradient boosting regression\nL61:   * XGBoosting regression\nL62:   * Support vector regression\nL63:   * Multi-layered perception\nL64: Machine learning models transcript.py\nL65: \nL66: A direct copy of the Jupyter Notebook, as a regular Python script.\nL67: \nL68: \nL69: \nL70: Main figure generator.Rmd\nL71: \nL72: R Markdown file that generates the figures from the paper's main text.\nL73: \nL74: \nL75: \nL76: Sensory data quality control - ANOVA on repeated samples.Rmd\nL77: \nL78: R Markdown file that performs ANOVA on the beer samples that were repeated in multiple sessions with the trained panel.\nL79: \nL80: \nL81: \nL82: Supplemental Figure generator.Rmd\nL83: \nL84: R Markdown file that generates the supplemental figures of the paper.\nL85: Supplemental Files and Figure source files.xlsx\nL86: \nL87: Excel file containing all the supplemental files and tables, and the raw data underlying every figure (main and supplemental) in the manuscript.\nL88: \nL89: \nL90: \nL91: Raw sensory data anonymized.csv\nL92: \nL93: A CSV file containing the raw, unprocessed scores of our panel per panelist for each beer.\nL94: \nL95: This corresponds to the anonymized version of \"Raw_Sensory_data_Trained_panel.csv\", which is referenced in one of the scripts.\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: \nL99: RateBeer_Appreciation_Gradient_Boost_Regressor.pkl\nL100: Pickled version of the best-performing model for predicting RateBeer Appreciation from chemical composition (Python version 3.9.16).\nL101: \nL102: \nL103: \nL104: RateBeer_Full_Model_Gradient_Boost_Regressor.pkl\nL105: \nL106: Pickled version of the best-performing model for predicting all RateBeer scores, from beer chemical composition (Python version 3.9.16).\nL107: \nL108: \nL109: \nL110: SHAP analysis.ipynb\nL111: Jupyter Notebook that performs SHAP analysis on the models. It also extracts the SHAP values in a CSV file to make a customized SHAP plot in R with the \"Main Figure Generator.Rmd\" script.\nL112: \nL113: \nL114: \nL115: Partial Dependence Plots with SHAP.ipynb\nL116: \nL117: Jupyter Notebook that generates Partial Dependence Plots of your model for compounds of your choice.\nL118: \nL119: \nL120: \nL121: \nL122: \nL123: SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS\nL124: \nL125: Any operating system capable of running Python v3.9.16 and R v4.2.2\nL126: \nL127: Python v3.9.16 packages:\nL128: anaconda    2023.07\nL129: conda    23.5.0\nL130: joblib    1.2.0\nL131: numpy    1.24.3\nL132: pandas    1.5.3\nL133: scikit-learn    1.2.2\nL134: seaborn    0.12.2\nL135: shap    0.41.0\nL136: xgboost    1.7.3\nL137: \nL138: R v4.2.2 packages:\nL139: factoextra    1.0.7\nL140: forcats    1.0.0\nL141: ggbeeswarm    0.7.1\nL142: ggpmisc    0.5.2\nL143: ggpubr    0.6.0\nL144: ggsignif    0.6.4\nL145: ggtext    0.1.2\nL146: grid    4.2.2\nL147: gridExtra    2.3\nL148: Hmisc    4.8.0\nL149: naniar    1.0.0\nL150: psycho    0.6.1\nL151: RColorBrewer    1.1.3\nL152: shapes    1.2.7\nL153: stats    4.2.2\nL154: svglite    2.1.1\nL155: tidyquant    1.0.6\nL156: tidyverse    2.0.0\nL157: \nL158: \nL159: \nL160: INSTALLATION GUIDE\nL161: Download and install Python from Python or Anaconda.\nL162: Download and install R from CRAN.\nL163: \nL164: Packages are installed with the built-in package installers.\nL165: \nL166: \nL167: \nL168: DEMO / INSTRUCTIONS FOR USE\nL169: \nL170: All available data is included in the file \"Supplemental Files and Figure source files.xlsx\".\nL171: Open the worksheet of your choise and save this as a .CSV file.\nL172: \nL173: All scripts refer to the corresponding worksheet names whenever the data is required.\nL174: The expected output is identical to the figures, tables and supplemental files presented in the manuscript.\nL175: ## Files\nL176: \nL177: ###\nL178: \nL179: README.txt\nL180: \nL181: 【15†Preview】\nL182: ###\nL183: \nL184: Files (52.7 MB)\nL185: Name  | Size  |\nL186: --- | --- | ---\nL187: 【16†init_modeling.py】 md5:c02fab431d33b40a06c0235cbbc61998  | 10.4 kB  | 【16†Download】\nL188: 【17†Jupyter Notebook for training machine learning models.ipynb】 md5:6b311c6c3575f8dc27070fbed7bf916a  | 250.4 kB  | 【18†Preview】 【17†Download】\nL189: 【19†Machine learning models transcript.py】 md5:06bffe872df9b6f109c6d8dca78ecfd7  | 34.7 kB  | 【19†Download】\nL190: 【20†Main Figure Generator.Rmd】 md5:ebaee5c79bd6f469a8141b3a7a65f77f  | 72.8 kB  | 【20†Download】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://zenodo.org/records/10653704/files/Sensory%20data%20quality%20control%20-%20ANOVA%20on%20repeated%20samples.Rmd?download=1)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 95\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://zenodo.org/records/10653704/files/Machine%20learning%20models%20transcript.py?download=1)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view3\",\"lineno\":68}); Total lines: 732\nL0: #%% md\nL1: \nL2: # Importing packages\nL3: #%%\nL4: import os\nL5: from platform import system\nL6: import pandas as pd\nL7: import numpy as np\nL8: from numpy.random import randint\nL9: \nL10: from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nL11: from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nL12: \nL13: from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nL14: from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nL15: from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nL16: from sklearn.model_selection import KFold\nL17: from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nL18: from sklearn.linear_model import LassoCV, MultiTaskLassoCV\nL19: from sklearn.cross_decomposition import PLSRegression\nL20: \nL21: from sklearn.multioutput import MultiOutputRegressor\nL22: \nL23: from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nL24: from sklearn.ensemble import ExtraTreesRegressor\nL25: from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor\nL26: from sklearn.ensemble import AdaBoostRegressor\nL27: \nL28: from xgboost import XGBRegressor\nL29: \nL30: from sklearn.svm import SVR\nL31: from sklearn.neural_network import MLPRegressor\nL32: \nL33: import optuna\nL34: \nL35: import pickle\nL36: import joblib\nL37: \nL38: #%% md\nL39: #### Working directory\nL40: #%%\nL41: \nL42: ### TO DO: ADJUST TO YOUR RELEVANT WORKING DIRECTORY\nL43: os.chdir(os.path.join('my', 'directory'))\nL44: \nL45: np.random.seed(42)\nL46: #%%\nL47: from init_modeling import generate_X\nL48: from init_modeling import regressor_performance, pickle_model\nL49: #%% md\nL50: # Creating required folders\nL51: #%%\nL52: for path in ['models', 'Model_performance', 'Optimal_settings']:\nL53:     if not os.path.exists(path):\nL54:         os.makedirs(path)\nL55: #%% md\nL56: #### Test train split\nL57: # Comment/uncomment one of both sections to select the RateBeer or the Trained Panel sensory datasets.\nL58: # Note that the RateBeer dataset is not available without prior written permission from RateBeer (ZX Ventures, USA)\nL59: #%%\nL60: # chem_dataset_ratebeer = pd.read_csv('chem_dataset_ratebeer.csv').set_index('beer')\nL61: # x_name = 'chem_log'\nL62: # X = generate_X(pd.concat([chem_dataset_ratebeer['beer_id'], chem_dataset_ratebeer.loc[:,'acetaldehyde':'sulfur_sum']], axis=1), impute=True)\nL63: # y_name = 'ratebeer'\nL64: # Y = chem_dataset_ratebeer.loc[:,'ave_score_5':'overall_20']\nL65: # y_class = chem_dataset_ratebeer.loc[:,'tasting_category_fine']\nL66: # X_train, X_test, Y_train, Y_test = train_test_split(X, Y, test_size=0.30,\nL67: #                                                     random_state=0, shuffle=True, stratify=y_class)\nL68: chem_dataset = pd.read_csv('Supplemental File S1.csv').set_index('beer')\nL69: trained_panel_dataset = pd.read_csv('Supplemental File S4.csv').set_index('beer')\nL70: chem_dataset_expertpanel = chem_dataset.join(trained_panel_dataset)\nL71: x_name = 'chem_log'\nL72: X = generate_X(pd.concat([chem_dataset_expertpanel['beer_id'] , chem_dataset_expertpanel.loc[:,'acetaldehyde':'sulfur_sum']], axis=1), impute=True)\nL73: y_name = 'expertpanel'\nL74: Y = chem_dataset_expertpanel.loc[:,'A_malt_all':'overall']\nL75: y_class = chem_dataset_expertpanel.loc[:,'tasting_category_fine']\nL76: X_train, X_test, Y_train, Y_test = train_test_split(X, Y, test_size=0.30,\nL77:                                                     random_state=0, shuffle=True, stratify=y_class)\nL78: #%% md\nL79: #### Scaling the data\nL80: # This is necessary for the Neural Network and Linear Models. Out of consistency, we do it for the whole dataset.\nL81: #%%\nL82: scaler_X = StandardScaler().fit(X_train)\nL83: # scaler_Y = StandardScaler().fit(Y_train)\nL84: X_train = scaler_X.transform(X_train)\nL85: X_test = scaler_X.transform(X_test)\nL86: # Y_train = scaler_Y.transform(Y_train)\nL87: # Y_test = scaler_Y.transform(Y_test)\nL88: #%% md\nL89: # Creating first order interaction terms\nL90: #%%\nL91: poly = PolynomialFeatures(interaction_only=True)\nL92: \nL93: X_train_int = poly.fit_transform(X_train)\nL94: X_test_int = poly.fit_transform(X_test)\nL95: \nL96: X_train_int = pd.DataFrame(X_train_int,columns=poly.get_feature_names_out()).drop('1', axis = 1)\nL97: X_test_int = pd.DataFrame(X_test_int,columns=poly.get_feature_names_out()).drop('1', axis = 1)\nL98: #%% md\nL99: ### Linear model\nL100: #%%\nL101: performance_loop = []\nL102: for aspect in Y_train.columns:\nL103:     Y_train_loop = Y_train[aspect]\nL104:     Y_test_loop = Y_test[aspect]\nL105: \nL106:     regressor_loop = LinearRegression(fit_intercept=True, copy_X=True)\nL107:     regressor_loop.fit(X_train_int, Y_train_loop)\nL108:     mse_train, r2_train, mse_test, r2_test = regressor_performance(X_train_int, Y_train_loop, X_test_int, Y_test_loop, regressor_loop, toprint=False)\nL109:     performance_loop.append(['LinearModel interactions', x_name, y_name, aspect, mse_train, r2_train, np.sqrt(r2_train), mse_test, r2_test, np.sqrt(r2_test)])\nL110: \nL111:     joblib.dump(regressor_loop, os.path.join('models', f'Linear_regression_interaction_{aspect}_{y_name}.pkl'))\nL112: \nL113: # Training full model\nL114: LM = LinearRegression(fit_intercept=True, copy_X=True).fit(X_train_int, Y_train)\nL115: mse_train, r2_train, mse_test, r2_test = regressor_performance(X_train_int, Y_train, X_test_int, Y_test, LM, toprint = False)\nL116: aspect = 'everything'\nL117: \nL118: joblib.dump(LM, os.path.join('models', f'Linear_regression_interaction_full_model_{y_name}.pkl'))\nL119: \nL120: # Adding full model to the list\nL121: performance_loop.append(['Complete_LinearModel interactions', x_name, y_name, aspect, mse_train, r2_train, np.sqrt(r2_train), mse_test, r2_test, np.sqrt(r2_test)])\nL122: \nL123: # Turning the list into a dataframe\nL124: performance_lr_df = pd.DataFrame(performance_loop,\nL125:                                  columns=['regressor','X', 'Y', 'aspect', 'mse_train', 'r2_train', 'r_train', 'mse_test', 'r2_test', 'r_test'])\nL126: \nL127: # Saving\nL128: performance_lr_df.to_csv(f'Model_performance/ModelPerformances_{x_name}_{y_name}_Linear_model_interactions.csv', index=False)\nL129: #%% md\nL130: ### Lasso regression\nL131: #%%\nL132: performance_loop = []\nL133: for aspect in Y_train.columns:\nL134:     Y_train_loop = Y_train[aspect]\nL135:     Y_test_loop = Y_test[aspect]\nL136: \nL137:     regressor_loop = LassoCV(cv = 5, random_state = 0)\nL138:     regressor_loop.fit(X_train_int, Y_train_loop)\nL139:     mse_train, r2_train, mse_test, r2_test = regressor_performance(X_train_int, Y_train_loop, X_test_int, Y_test_loop, regressor_loop, toprint=False)\nL140:     performance_loop.append(['Lasso interactions', x_name, y_name, aspect, mse_train, r2_train, np.sqrt(r2_train), mse_test, r2_test, np.sqrt(r2_test)])\nL141: \nL142:     joblib.dump(regressor_loop, os.path.join('models', f'Lasso_regression_interactions_{aspect}_{y_name}.pkl'))\nL143: \nL144: # Training full model\nL145: lasso_cv = MultiTaskLassoCV(cv = 5, random_state = 0).fit(X_train_int, Y_train)\nL146: mse_train, r2_train, mse_test, r2_test = regressor_performance(X_train_int, Y_train, X_test_int, Y_test, lasso_cv, toprint = False)\nL147: aspect = 'everything'\nL148: \nL149: joblib.dump(lasso_cv, os.path.join('models', f'Lasso_regression_interactions_full_model_{y_name}.pkl'))\nL150: \nL151: # Adding full model to the list\nL152: performance_loop.append(['Complete_Lasso interactions', x_name, y_name, aspect, mse_train, r2_train, np.sqrt(r2_train), mse_test, r2_test, np.sqrt(r2_test)])\nL153: \nL154: # Turning the list into a dataframe\nL155: performance_l1r_df = pd.DataFrame(performance_loop,\nL156:                                   columns=['regressor','X', 'Y', 'aspect', 'mse_train', 'r2_train', 'r_train', 'mse_test', 'r2_test', 'r_test'])\nL157: \nL158: # Saving\nL159: performance_l1r_df.to_csv(f'Model_performance/ModelPerformances_{x_name}_{y_name}_Lasso_interactions.csv', index=False)\nL160: #%% md\nL161: ### Partial Least Squares\nL162: \nL163: # For PLSR, grid search returned either the same or worse models. So we are using the native model.\nL164: #%%\nL165: performance_loop = []\nL166: for aspect in Y_train.columns:\nL167:     Y_train_loop = Y_train[aspect]\nL168:     Y_test_loop = Y_test[aspect]\nL169: \nL170:     regressor_loop = PLSRegression(scale=True)\nL171:     regressor_loop.fit_transform(X_train, Y_train_loop)\nL172:     mse_train, r2_train, mse_test, r2_test = regressor_performance(X_train, Y_train_loop, X_test, Y_test_loop, regressor_loop, toprint=True)\nL173:     performance_loop.append(['PLS', x_name, y_name, aspect, mse_train, r2_train, np.sqrt(r2_train), mse_test, r2_test, np.sqrt(r2_test)])\nL174: \nL175: \nL176:     joblib.dump(regressor_loop, os.path.join('models', f'PLS_regression_{aspect}_{y_name}.pkl'))\nL177: \nL178: # Training full model\nL179: PLS = PLSRegression().fit(X_train, Y_train)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://zenodo.org/records/10653704/files/Main%20Figure%20Generator.Rmd?download=1)\nciteturn9view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"lineno\":658}); Total lines: 1817\nL568:               labels_row = clean_labels_x,\nL569:               labels_col = clean_labels_y,\nL570:               border_color = \"white\",\nL571:               annotation_colors = annot_colors,\nL572:               annotation_names_row = FALSE,\nL573:               annotation_names_col = FALSE,\nL574:               annotation_row = annotation_x,\nL575:               annotation_col = annotation_y,\nL576:               scale = \"none\",\nL577:               drop_levels = TRUE,\nL578:               gaps_row = c(5,10,15,20,25,30,33,39,42,45,49),\nL579:               gaps_col = c(11,16,24,26,35),\nL580:               angle_col = 90,\nL581:               legend_breaks = c(-0.85, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 0.85),\nL582:               legend_labels = c(\"-0.85\",\"-0.50\",\" 0.00\",\" 0.50\",\" 0.85\"),\nL583:               legend = TRUE,\nL584:               breaks = breaksList,\nL585:               fontsize = 6,\nL586:               fontsize_row = 5,\nL587:               fontsize_col = 5,\nL588:               na_col = \"white\")\nL589: ```\nL590: \nL591: ```{r}\nL592: save_pheatmap_svg(g,\nL593:                   'chemical_sensory_white_borders.svg',\nL594:                   width = 8,\nL595:                   height = 8)\nL596: ```\nL597: \nL598: ### Correlation tables\nL599: \nL600: ```{r}\nL601: corr_matrix_all <- cor(x = data_all, y = data_all,\nL602:                    use = \"pairwise.complete.obs\", method = c(\"spearman\"))\nL603: corr_table <- corr_matrix_all[heatmap_chem_subset %>% select(ethyl_acetate:kcalperc) %>% colnames(),\nL604:                               all_ep %>% select(A_malt_all:overall) %>% colnames()]\nL605: \nL606: clean_labels_x <- sensory_names %>%\nL607:   slice(match(colnames(corr_table), R_name)) %>%\nL608:   pull(CleanName_PanelFeedback)\nL609: \nL610: row.names(corr_table) <- clean_labels_y\nL611: colnames(corr_table) <- clean_labels_x\nL612: \nL613: write.csv(corr_table,\nL614:           'correlation_chemical_sensory.csv',\nL615:           row.names = TRUE)\nL616: ```\nL617: \nL618: # Validation results\nL619: \nL620: ```{r}\nL621: library(tidyverse)\nL622: library(ggpubr)\nL623: library(naniar)\nL624: library(stats)\nL625: library(shapes)\nL626: library(psycho)\nL627: library(ggsignif)\nL628: library(forcats)\nL629: library(grid)\nL630: library(ggtext)\nL631: library(svglite)\nL632: ```\nL633: \nL634: ### Variables\nL635: \nL636: ```{r}\nL637: print <- FALSE\nL638: color_control <- \"#FFC2D1\"\nL639: color_spiked <- \"#C72468\"\nL640: \nL641: color_yeast <- \"#E86A3B\"\nL642: color_malt <- \"#6BA8C9\"\nL643: color_hop <- \"#ABD17D\"\nL644: color_wildflora <- \"#FACA13\"\nL645: color_other <- \"#5A5F62\"\nL646: palette <- c(Malt = color_malt,\nL647:              Hops = color_hop,\nL648:              Yeast = color_yeast,\nL649:              Spices = color_other,\nL650:              Others = color_other,\nL651:              \"Wild flora\" = color_wildflora)\nL652: \nL653: color_beer <- \"#054a91\"\nL654: color_darkbeer <- \"#f17300\"\nL655: blank <- grid.rect(gp = gpar(col = \"white\"))\nL656: ```\nL657: \nL658: ### Functions\nL659: \nL660: ```{r}\nL661: scale_na <- function(x){\nL662:   if(is.na(sd(x, na.rm = TRUE))){\nL663:     x_scaled <- NA\nL664:   }\nL665:   else{\nL666:     x_scaled <- (x - mean(x, na.rm = TRUE))/(sd(x, na.rm = TRUE)^as.logical(sd(x, na.rm = TRUE)))\nL667:   }\nL668:   return(x_scaled)\nL669: }\nL670: \nL671: \nL672: ## Indicate if each entry is an outlier\nL673: is_outlier <- function(x) {\nL674:   lower_inner_fence <- quantile(x, 0.25, na.rm = TRUE) - 1.5 * IQR(x, na.rm = TRUE)\nL675:   upper_inner_fence <- quantile(x, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE) + 1.5 * IQR(x, na.rm = TRUE)\nL676:   outlier_state <- ifelse(x < lower_inner_fence, \"lower\",\nL677:                           ifelse(x > upper_inner_fence, \"upper\", NA))\nL678:   return(outlier_state)\nL679: }\nL680: \nL681: \nL682: std <- function(x) {\nL683:   sd(x)/sqrt(length(x))\nL684: }\nL685: \nL686: conf_int_low <- function(x){\nL687:   t <- t.test(x)\nL688:   return(t$conf.int[1][1])\nL689: }\nL690: \nL691: conf_int_high <- function(x){\nL692:   t <- t.test(x)\nL693:   return(t$conf.int[2][1])\nL694: }\nL695: \nL696: binom_pval <- function(x, n){\nL697:   b <- binom.test(x = x, n = n, alternative = \"t\")\nL698:   return(b$p.value)\nL699: }\nL700: binom_cil <- function(x, n){\nL701:   b <- binom.test(x = x, n = n, alternative = \"t\")\nL702:   return(b$conf.int[1])\nL703: }\nL704: \nL705: binom_ciu <- function(x, n){\nL706:   b <- binom.test(x = x, n = n, alternative = \"t\")\nL707:   return(b$conf.int[2])\nL708: }\nL709: ```\nL710: \nL711: ### Loading data\nL712: \nL713: ```{r}\nL714: validation_200713 <- read.csv(\"Validation Results 1.csv\")\nL715: validation_200803 <- read.csv(\"Validation Results 2.csv\")\nL716: validation_200805 <- read.csv(\"Validation Results 3.csv\")\nL717: \nL718: beer_names <- read.csv(\"Validation Details.csv\")\nL719: spiking_concentrations <- read.csv(\"Validation Concentrations.csv\") #%>%\nL720:   #mutate(SetName = gsub(\"-\",\"-\",SetName))\nL721:   #mutate(SetName = gsub(\"\\U2014\",\"-\",SetName))\nL722: \nL723: thresholds <- read.csv(\"Sensory thresholds.csv\")\nL724: ```\nL725: \nL726: ### Preprocessing\nL727: \nL728: ```{r}\nL729: validation_results  <- validation_200713 %>%\nL730:   #mutate() %>%\nL731:   bind_rows(validation_200803) %>%\nL732:   #mutate() %>%\nL733:   separate(Time, c(\"Date\",\"Time\"), sep = \" \") %>%\nL734:   left_join(beer_names %>% select(Date, BeerStyle, SetName, Code, SampleName), c(\"Date\" = \"Date\", \"Beer\" = \"Code\")) %>%\nL735:   mutate(Beer = SampleName) %>%\nL736:   select(BeerStyle:SampleName, Name:Overall.appreciation) %>%\nL737:   distinct() %>%\nL738:   gather(Sensory_profile, Score, Malt:Overall.appreciation)\nL739: sensory_clean <- data.frame(Sensory_profile = validation_results %>% distinct(Sensory_profile),\nL740:                             CleanName = c(\"Malt flavor\",\"Sweetness\",\"Hop flavor\",\"Bitterness\",\nL741:                                           \"Ester flavor\",\"Body fullness\",\"Alcohol\",\"Overall appreciation\")) %>%\nL742:   mutate(CleanName = factor(CleanName,\nL743:                             levels = c(\"Malt flavor\",\"Hop flavor\",\"Ester flavor\",\nL744:                                        \"Sweetness\",\"Bitterness\",\"Alcohol\",\"Body fullness\",\nL745:                                        \"Overall appreciation\")))\nL746: validation_results <- validation_results %>%\nL747:   left_join(sensory_clean, by = c(\"Sensory_profile\" = \"Sensory_profile\"))\nL748: validation_results_mean <- validation_results %>%\nL749:   group_by(SetName, Beer, CleanName) %>%\nL750:   summarize_at(vars(Score), list(Mean = mean, StdErr = std, sd = sd,\nL751:                                  confintlow = conf_int_low, confinthigh = conf_int_high))\nL752: \nL753: validation_results_diff <- validation_results %>%\nL754:   select(-Sensory_profile, -BeerStyle, -SampleName) %>%\nL755:   spread(Beer, Score) %>%\nL756:   mutate(Difference = Spiked - Control)\nL757: ```\nL758: \nL759: TREAT AS FORCED CHOICE CATEGORICAL DATA\nL760: \nL761: We're not looking at a 'true mean' but the difference between the two\nL762: \nL763: ```{r}\nL764: validation_results_freechoice <- validation_results_diff  %>%\nL765:   filter(Difference != 0) %>%\nL766:   bind_rows(validation_results_diff  %>%\nL767:               filter(Difference == 0) %>%\nL768:               arrange(CleanName) %>%\nL769:               mutate(Difference = ifelse(row_number() %% 2 == 0, -1, 1))) %>%\nL770:   mutate(Group = ifelse(Difference > 0, \"Spiked\", \"Control\"))\nL771: validation_results_freechoice_counts <- validation_results_freechoice %>%\nL772:   select(SetName, Name, CleanName, Group) %>%\nL773:   count(SetName, CleanName, Group)\nL774: validation_results_freechoice_ratio <- validation_results_freechoice_counts %>%\nL775:   spread(Group, n) %>%\nL776:   mutate(Ratio = Spiked/(Control + Spiked)) %>%\nL777:   group_by(SetName) %>%\nL778:   rowwise() %>%\nL779:   mutate(pval = binom_pval(Spiked, n = (Spiked + Control))) #%>%\nL780: #mutate(ci_lower = binom_cil(Spiked, n = (Spiked + Control))) %>%\nL781: #mutate(ci_upper = binom_ciu(Spiked, n = (Spiked + Control)))\nL782: ```\nL783: \nL784: ```{r}\nL785: validation_results_freechoice_signif_scaled_blond  <- validation_results_freechoice_ratio %>%\nL786:   select(-Ratio) %>%\nL787:   mutate(Count_total = Control + Spiked) %>%\nL788:   mutate(SetName = paste0(SetName, ' (n = ', Count_total, ')')) %>%\nL789:   gather(Group, Count, Control:Spiked) %>%\nL790:   mutate(Count_scaled = Count/Count_total) %>%\nL791:   group_by(SetName, CleanName) %>%\nL792:   mutate(y_position = max(Count_scaled) + 0.08) %>%\nL793:   bind_rows(data.frame(SetName = c(\"Best predictors (n = 20)\",\"Best predictors (n = 20)\",\"Best predictors - no ethanol (n = 13)\",\"Best predictors - no ethanol (n = 13)\"),\nL794:                        \"CleanName\" = c(\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\"),\nL795:                        Group = c(\"Control\",\"Spiked\",\"Control\",\"Spiked\"),\nL796:                        Count = c(0,0))) %>%\nL797:   mutate(CleanName = factor(CleanName,\nL798:                             levels = c(\"Malt flavor\",\"Hop flavor\",\"Ester flavor\",\nL799:                                        \"Sweetness\",\"Bitterness\",\"Alcohol\",\"Body fullness\",\"\",\nL800:                                        \"Overall appreciation\"))) %>%\nL801:   ungroup()\nL802: ```\nL803: \nL804: Spiking concentrations - calculate percentage increase?\nL805: \nL806: ```{r}\n"
